A||||10||AkursedX||Engine Bay Cooling Help!  (Free too!)||||14||
Z||000000||AkursedX||06-22-2001||10:05 PM||||Well, like many other Fiero owners, I have been look for cheap and easy solutions to keep my engine bay a little cooler, I have run across a easy little thing to do.  <P>Well 1st, I wanted to drill out all the rivets in the engine grilles, but I deducted that alot of crap would get into engine bay in the fall and spring, and the only way I could do that is if I had another set of grilles to keep as stock.<P>So, I did the next best thing!  On the advice of Grinthock at our weekly Fiero Get-togethers in Hamilton, I took the little fins on the underneath of the engine grilles, and I bent them all upwards so it would make air easier to escape.  It took about 10 minutes with a pair of needlenose pliers.  And by my rough estimates, I figure that I doubled the amount of hot air that could escape.<P>Well, I must say that this has helped my cooling situation dramatically!  I have been running cooler in every type of driving!  And it didn't cost me a dime!!<P>Plus, if it starts getting too messy in the engine bay, I could always bend them back.<P>I just figured I would get this little tip out to everyone, hopefully it'll help some of you out!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||Bazooka||06-23-2001||10:04 AM||||Akursed,<BR>    You offer an nice way to bring additional airflow out of the engine bay. In my case I chose not to bend the fins on the liner below. Instead I drilled out the 8 or 9 rivets and then I drilled and tapped for small screws. That way, during racing weather I can remove the smaller fins on the underside of the deck vents and reinstall them during the fall and winter to avoid leaves, etc. You can hear the difference on the K&N air intake filter and sound difference for velocity. It's like night and day. Since I have a ZZ4 (V8), engine cooling air is more than basic.  <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A>  <BR>****************************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AkursedX:<BR><B>Well, like many other Fiero owners, I have been look for cheap and easy solutions to keep my engine bay a little cooler, I have run across a easy little thing to do.  <BR>Well 1st, I wanted to drill out all the rivets in the engine grilles, but I deducted that alot of crap would get into engine bay in the fall and spring, and the only way I could do that is if I had another set of grilles to keep as stock.<BR>So, I did the next best thing!  On the advice of Grinthock at our weekly Fiero Get-togethers in Hamilton, I took the little fins on the underneath of the engine grilles, and I bent them all upwards so it would make air easier to escape.  It took about 10 minutes with a pair of needlenose pliers.  And by my rough estimates, I figure that I doubled the amount of hot air that could escape.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||06-23-2001||10:34 AM||||Good idea. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I bent mine up a couple of months ago and found out that they were pretty brittle.  A few of them couldn't handle the stress and snapped off.  I was bending them pretty close to the ends though.  They happened to break in a pattern of every other one so I took out every other one to make it look normal.  They still have decent protection from leaves and I clean out what might or did make it through when I wash my car.  I almost went with the expanded metal grill idea, but this will work for now. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-23-2001||10:58 AM||||Thanks for the idea! <P>What if i drilled the rivits out completely like Bazooka, remove it completely, and put a wire mesh there to stop leaves etc?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||06-23-2001||01:01 PM||||I decided to drill mine out completly a while back. I was worried, but now couldn't be happier. I might put some screen in there to keep stuff out, but I'll see if I really need it first. Another thing I did was ground the relay for the engine bay cooling fan. So, it is now on all the time the key is on. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jake2m4||06-24-2001||01:38 AM||||Just a suggestion...<P>I wouldn't remove the mesh from the bottom of the grills.  I left the engine grills off of mine for a few weeks while working on it.  Later when I was warming it up, I found pine-straw burning on the exhaust pipes.  So removing the mesh from the grill might cause some of the same problems.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero Sport Coupe<BR>'95 Pontiac Grand AM SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||06-24-2001||11:42 AM||||Maybe what we need, is a fine screen on <B>top</B> of the grills, to keep stuff out of there in the first place?  You could brush off any leaves before you started out, or just let 'em blow off.  Trouble is, that would look terrible... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>How about venetian blinds?  You'd want them to open up when you started the car, and shut when you turned it off... big vacuum actuator with delayed closer, or maybe even operate them by oil pressure - ?<P>That's enough food for thought.  Let me know, when you guys get all the details worked out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   Oh, and none of these would be free... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Sorry, off topic... ||||reg||3
Z||000007||84Bill||06-24-2001||11:48 AM||||I've been trying to bend the fins on my 84's covers and I gotta tell you it's not an easy task  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. Is there any other way to cool an 84 with solid covers?||||reg||12
Z||000008||AkursedX||06-24-2001||02:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>I've been trying to bend the fins on my 84's covers and I gotta tell you it's not an easy task   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. Is there any other way to cool an 84 with solid covers?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>But a pair of coupe grilles from another year.  Then use them in conjunction with the center grille.  you will get alot more cooling!<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||theogre||06-24-2001||03:18 PM||||Another option... get some decorative stamped aluminum "screen" from a hardware store. Or install the old style screen under the new style grate. (The old screen has 1/4 hex shaped holes punched over the whole area.)<P>On the 84 deck lid.... the under screen was suposed to be removed during recall. You could replace it with the material above. That would open things up.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||06-24-2001||07:15 PM||||I took out the screen underneath the grills on my GT after I bought the car over a year ago.. haven't had any problems with debris piling up on the exhaust or anything.||||reg||1
